#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Multi-Out (macOS) — route audio to several outputs at once.

The macOS port of the Linux Multi-Out. Where the Linux build stitched a
PipeWire combine sink together, this one builds a CoreAudio *Multi-Output
device* (a non-stacked aggregate) over whichever outputs you tick and makes it
the system default, so the same audio plays on Bluetooth, built-in, HDMI and
AirPlay together. Tick one output and it just becomes the default directly —
no aggregate is built.

Run with --daemon for the headless auto-switch agent (a launchd LaunchAgent):
it watches CoreAudio and points audio at a Bluetooth device the moment one
connects, folding it into any multi-output you already have running.

CoreAudio is reached two ways, on purpose:
  * ctypes against the CoreAudio framework for every AudioObject property
    read/write — deterministic pointer handling across OS and PyObjC versions.
  * PyObjC (Foundation) only to assemble the aggregate-device description
    dictionary, where a hand-rolled CFDictionary would be needlessly fragile.

Requires: macOS, python3, PyObjC (`pip install pyobjc-core pyobjc-framework-Cocoa`),
and PyQt6 for the window (`pip install PyQt6`). The --daemon mode needs neither
PyQt6 nor a display.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

